# Prokka v1.14.6 – Genome Annotation

## Description

Prokka is a bacterial genome annotation tool used to identify coding sequences, tRNAs, rRNAs, and other genomic features. It generates annotated files in GFF, GenBank, FASTA, and tabular formats.

Prokka v1.14.6 was used to annotate the *Providencia stuartii* genome assemblies before pan-genome analysis.

## 1. Installation

```bash
mamba create -n prokka_env -c conda-forge -c bioconda prokka=1.14.6
conda activate prokka_env

prokka --version
```

## 2. Input Preparation

Organize the genome assemblies in a directory:

```text
Providencia_Project/
├── Genomes/
│   ├── Genome_01.fna
│   ├── Genome_02.fna
│   └── ...
└── Prokka_Results/
```

Create the output directory:

```bash
mkdir -p Prokka_Results
```

## 3. Annotation of a Single Genome

```bash
prokka \
--outdir Prokka_Results/Genome_01 \
--prefix Genome_01 \
--kingdom Bacteria \
--genus Providencia \
--species stuartii \
--strain Genome_01 \
--gram neg \
--cpus 8 \
Genomes/Genome_01.fna
```

## 4. Batch Annotation of All Genomes

Create a script:

```bash
nano run_prokka.sh
```

Add the following commands:

```bash
#!/bin/bash

INPUT_DIR="Genomes"
OUTPUT_DIR="Prokka_Results"

mkdir -p "$OUTPUT_DIR"

for genome in "$INPUT_DIR"/*.fna
do
    sample=$(basename "$genome" .fna)

    echo "Annotating $sample"

    prokka \
    --outdir "$OUTPUT_DIR/$sample" \
    --prefix "$sample" \
    --kingdom Bacteria \
    --genus Providencia \
    --species stuartii \
    --strain "$sample" \
    --gram neg \
    --cpus 8 \
    "$genome"

done

echo "Genome annotation completed."
```

Run the script:

```bash
chmod +x run_prokka.sh
./run_prokka.sh
```

## 5. Output Files

| File | Description |
|---|---|
| `.gff` | Annotated genomic features |
| `.gbk` | GenBank annotation |
| `.faa` | Predicted protein sequences |
| `.ffn` | Predicted gene nucleotide sequences |
| `.fna` | Genomic nucleotide sequences |
| `.tsv` | Tabular annotation |
| `.txt` | Annotation statistics |
| `.log` | Execution log |

The generated GFF files are used as input for the subsequent Roary pan-genome analysis.

